---
import Callout from "../../components/Callout.astro";
import PageShell from "../../components/PageShell.astro";
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
---

<MainLayout title="Limitations - AI Training Guide">
	<PageShell chapterId="competitive-research" subChapterId="limitations">
		<div class="prose space-y-6">
			<p>
				Think of AI as a new hire on their first week. They're eager, they work
				fast, and they have broad general knowledge, but they don't know the
				company, the client's market, or the context behind the client's decisions. Everything
				they produce needs a review pass from someone who does. Treat AI output
				as a strong starting point, not a finished product.
			</p>

			<p>
				Beyond that general framing, there are some specific limitations worth
				knowing about so you can work around them rather than getting tripped
				up by them.
			</p>

			<h3>Known Limitations</h3>

			<ul class="space-y-4 my-4">
				<li>
					<span class="font-semibold">Knowledge cutoff.</span> AI models are
					trained on data up to a certain date. Without web search enabled,
					they have no awareness of anything that happened after that cutoff.
					Recent competitor launches, pricing changes, or market shifts will
					be missing entirely.
				</li>
				<li>
					<span class="font-semibold">Limited to public data.</span> AI only
					knows what was publicly available on the internet during its
					training. Internal documents, proprietary research, private Slack
					conversations, gated content behind logins: none of that exists to
					the AI unless you provide it directly.
				</li>
				<li>
					<span class="font-semibold">Numbers and statistics.</span> Modern
					models are far better at arithmetic than they used to be, often
					running calculations in code. The research risk is different: they
					pull the wrong figure from a source, misread a pricing tier, or state
					a market statistic that was never real. Any number headed for a
					deliverable should be traced back to a source you verified, especially
					in financial analysis or pricing comparisons.
				</li>
				<li>
					<span class="font-semibold">Niche topics.</span> The more niche your
					market or competitors, the less reliable the AI's knowledge. It will
					have plenty to say about Salesforce or Slack, but a smaller regional
					competitor may get sparse or inaccurate coverage.
				</li>
				<li>
					<span class="font-semibold">Confidence without certainty.</span> AI
					presents everything with the same confident tone whether it's correct
					or guessing. It won't flag when it's less sure about something, so
					you need to bring that critical eye yourself.
				</li>
				<li>
					<span class="font-semibold">Context window limits.</span> Long
					research sessions will eventually hit the AI's context window. When
					this happens, earlier parts of the conversation may get compressed
					or forgotten. Save findings to files as you go so you don't lose
					work.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<Callout variant="tip" title="Practical tip">
				<p>
					The best way to work with these limitations is to stay in the
					driver's seat. Use the AI to do the heavy lifting on gathering and
					organizing, then apply your own judgment for accuracy, nuance, and
					anything that requires insider knowledge. It's a research assistant,
					not a research department.
				</p>
			</Callout>
		</div>
	</PageShell>
</MainLayout>
